# The stable vanity URLs. Cloudflare's static-assets runtime reads this file from
# the build output; Astro copies public/ into dist/ verbatim.
#
# 302 throughout, never 301: every destination here is expected to move (a
# different host, a mirror, a new payment link), and a browser caches a permanent
# redirect long after the decision behind it has changed. These paths are the
# stable half; what they point at is not.

# The update feed. This one is not a convenience: the app's update check reads
# armada.mgcrea.io/appcast.xml, and that URL is compiled into every binary ever
# shipped, so it has to be one we can repoint without asking anyone to update
# first. `latest/download` also means publishing a release never needs a site
# deploy.
/appcast.xml  https://github.com/mgcrea/armada/releases/latest/download/appcast.xml  302

# The one download URL, on the same reasoning. It points at `latest/download`
# rather than a versioned asset, which only works because the release asset is
# named Armada.zip with no version in it.
/download     https://github.com/mgcrea/armada/releases/latest/download/Armada.zip  302
/checksum     https://github.com/mgcrea/armada/releases/latest/download/Armada.zip.sha256  302
/releases     https://github.com/mgcrea/armada/releases  302

# No /buy yet. It is added here when the Stripe payment link exists, as a 302 for
# the same reason as the rest: the app's licence pane will link to /buy, and a
# Stripe URL compiled into a shipped binary could never be repointed.
#
# Until that line exists, SHIPPED stays false in src/config.ts, and nothing on the
# site links to /buy. Flip the two in the same commit: a Buy button in front of a
# missing redirect is a 404 at the exact moment someone wanted to pay.
